      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.104,
            0.207,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "PROBABILITY"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.862,
            0.104,
            0.898,
            0.12
          ],
          "content": "211"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.138,
            0.795,
            0.158
          ],
          "content": "Let E be the event that ‘the music is stopped within the first half-minute’."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.17,
            0.796,
            0.208
          ],
          "content": "The outcomes favourable to E are points on the number line from 0 to $\\frac { 1 } { 2 }$ ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.206,
            0.742,
            0.248
          ],
          "content": "The distance from 0 to 2 is 2, while the distance from 0 to $\\frac { 1 } { 2 }$ is $\\frac { 1 } { 2 }$ ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.251,
            0.9,
            0.317
          ],
          "content": "Since all the outcomes are equally likely, we can argue that, of the total distance of 2, the distance favourable to the event E is $\\frac { 1 } { 2 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.364,
            0.135,
            0.382
          ],
          "content": "So,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.221,
            0.329,
            0.793,
            0.396
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{E})=\\frac{\\text { Distance forourable to the event E }}{\\text { Total distance in which outcomes can lie }}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\frac{1}{4}}",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.407,
            0.902,
            0.446
          ],
          "content": "Can we now extend the idea of Example 10 for finding the probability as the ratio of the favourable area to the total area?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.458,
            0.901,
            0.516
          ],
          "content": "**Example 11\\* :** A missing helicopter is reported to have crashed somewhere in the rectangular region shown in Fig. 14.2. What is the probability that it crashed inside the lake shown in the figure?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.196,
            0.519,
            0.837,
            0.789
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_9_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.966,
            0.563,
            0.983
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.799,
            0.807,
            0.874
          ],
          "content": "**Solution :** The helicopter is equally likely to crash anywhere in the region. Area of the entire region where the helicopter can crash $(4.5 \\times 9)    km ^2 = 40.5    km ^2$"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_footnote",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.882,
            0.467,
            0.902
          ],
          "content": "\\* Not from the examination point of view."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
